 # Front-to-Back Surface Extraction
-
-
 ## Overview
 Front-to-Back Surface Extraction is an image-order rendering technique that identifies the first voxel along a ray whose scalar value falls within a user-defined density threshold range, treating this point as the visible surface [S1, unofficial]. It discards all data behind the first hit [G].
-
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-- Input: A 3D scalar volume dataset $V$, a transfer function $T(d)$ mapping density to color, a threshold range $[\tau_{min}, \tau_{max}]$, a minimum gradient magnitude $\tau_{grad}$, camera parameters (origin $\mathbf{o}$, direction $\mathbf{d}$), and rendering parameters (step size, jitter factor, flags) [S1, unofficial].
+- Input: A 3D scalar volume dataset $V$, a transfer function $T(d)$ mapping density to color, a threshold range $[\tau_{\text{min}}, \tau_{\text{max}}]$, a minimum gradient magnitude $\tau_{\text{grad}}$, camera parameters (origin $\mathbf{o}$, direction $\mathbf{d}$), and rendering parameters (step size, jitter factor, flags) [S1, unofficial].
 - Output: A single pixel color $C$ and opacity $\alpha$ [S1, unofficial].
 
